(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["cs-12-06"] = {"1":{"s":"An addressing mode is the way an instruction tells where its data is. The 8085 has five. Immediate: the data is in the instruction (MVI A, 05H). Register: the data is in a register (MOV A, B). Direct: the address of the data is in the instruction (LDA 2000H). Register indirect: a register pair holds the address (MOV A, M). Implied: the instruction needs no operand (CMA).","w":["In register indirect mode, M means the memory location whose address is in the HL pair.","CMC and CMA name no operand, so they use implied addressing."],"x":["Every instruction needs to know where its data is. This is the addressing mode. A good choice of mode makes a program short and flexible.","Immediate addressing: the data is part of the instruction, for example MVI A, 05H puts 05H in the accumulator, and LXI H, 2000H puts 2000H in the HL pair. Register addressing: the data is in a register, for example MOV A, B copies B to A. Direct addressing: the instruction holds the address of the data, for example LDA 2000H loads the accumulator from location 2000H and STA 2001H stores it.","Register indirect addressing: a register pair holds the address of the data, for example MOV A, M copies the byte at the address in HL into A (here M is a memory location pointed to by HL); LDAX B uses the BC pair. Implied addressing: the data is fixed by the instruction itself and there is no operand, for example CMA (complement the accumulator), RLC and HLT."],"e":"LXI H, 2000H then MOV A, M reads the byte at 2000H: the HL pair is the pointer (register indirect). LDA 2000H does the same in one step (direct), but only for the accumulator."},"2":{"s":"The programming model shows the registers a programmer can use. The accumulator A and six 8-bit general purpose registers B, C, D, E, H and L, which can work as pairs BC, DE and HL. The flag register has five flags: S, Z, AC, P and CY. The 16-bit program counter holds the address of the next instruction and the 16-bit stack pointer holds the top of the stack.","w":["The temporary registers W and Z are not part of the programming model; the programmer cannot use them.","HL is also the pointer for the memory location called M."],"x":["The programming model lists the registers that can be used in programs. The accumulator A (8 bits) is the main register of the ALU: most arithmetic and logic results are kept in it. B, C, D, E, H and L are 8-bit general purpose registers that can also be used in pairs (BC, DE, HL) for 16-bit data and addresses.","The flag register has the five flags S (sign), Z (zero), AC (auxiliary carry), P (parity) and CY (carry), set or cleared by the result of arithmetic and logic instructions; the conditional jumps test them. The program counter (PC, 16 bits) holds the address of the next instruction. The stack pointer (SP, 16 bits) holds the address of the top of the stack.","The 16-bit address bus allows 64 KB of memory, and the accumulator, registers and flags together are what a program works with."],"e":"To add two numbers in memory: load the first into A, add the second with the ADD instruction, and the result is in A while the flags describe it."},"3":{"s":"The 8085 instructions are in groups. Data transfer copies data (MOV, MVI, LDA, STA, LXI, LHLD, SHLD, XCHG). Arithmetic: ADD, ADC, ADI, ACI, SUB, SBB, SUI, SBI, INR, DCR, INX, DCX, DAD, DAA. Logic: ANA, ORA, XRA, CMP and their immediate forms, CMA, CMC, STC and the rotates. Branching: JMP, conditional jumps, CALL, RET, RST. Stack: PUSH, POP. I/O: IN, OUT. Machine control: NOP, HLT, EI, DI, RIM, SIM.","w":["MOV and the other transfer instructions copy; the source keeps its value and no flag changes.","INX and DCX change no flag; INR and DCR change flags but not carry."],"x":["Data transfer instructions copy data between registers, memory and the accumulator without changing the source and without changing flags. MOV copies a register or memory byte, MVI puts an immediate byte, LDA and STA move the accumulator from or to a memory address, LXI loads a register pair, LHLD and SHLD load or store HL, XCHG exchanges HL with DE.","Arithmetic: add (ADD, ADC with carry, ADI, ACI), subtract (SUB, SBB with borrow, SUI, SBI), increment and decrement (INR, DCR for one register; INX, DCX for a pair), DAD (add a register pair to HL) and DAA (decimal adjust). Logic: AND, OR and XOR (ANA, ORA, XRA and ANI, ORI, XRI), compare (CMP, CPI: the result is only in the flags), complement (CMA, CMC), STC and the rotates RLC, RRC, RAL, RAR.","Branching changes the program counter. JMP jumps always; JC, JNC, JZ, JNZ, JP, JM, JPE, JPO jump if a flag condition is true; CALL and RET go to a subroutine and come back; RST n is a restart. Stack: PUSH saves a register pair on the stack and POP restores it. I/O: IN reads a port and OUT writes a port. Machine control: NOP does nothing, HLT stops, EI and DI enable and disable interrupts, RIM reads and SIM sets the interrupt masks and serial lines."],"e":"MVI A, 05H puts 05H in A; ADI 03H makes A equal 08H; STA 2000H stores it at 2000H."},"4":{"s":"An assembly language statement has four fields: label, mnemonic, operand and comment. A program loads the data, does the work with the instructions, stores the result and ends with HLT. Board programs: add two 8-bit or 16-bit numbers, arrange numbers in order, find the largest number in a block, add a block of numbers, count zeros, move a block of data, and take the 2s complement. The program should not exceed 20 to 29 instructions.","w":["Always end the program with HLT, or it keeps running.","Write the comments: they carry marks, and they show that you understand each instruction."],"x":["A statement has a label (a name for the address, ending with a colon, optional), a mnemonic (the instruction), an operand (data or address) and a comment (after a semicolon). Programs are written and then converted to machine code by an assembler.","Plan a program in four steps: load the data into registers, do the arithmetic or logic, store the result at the address asked, and stop with HLT. Use the carry flag to catch overflow and a counter register with a decrement and a conditional jump for loops.","Typical programs: 8-bit addition with carry (ADD, then JNC, INR); 16-bit addition (LHLD, XCHG, LHLD, DAD D, SHLD); arrange two numbers (CMP and a conditional jump); a block loop (LXI H for the pointer, MVI C for the count, then repeat with INX H, DCR C and JNZ); 2s complement (CMA then ADI 01H).","Check each program by tracing it with sample numbers: write the register values after every instruction. The Code lab in this app runs your program and shows the registers, the flags and the memory."],"e":"Count how many numbers in a block of 5 are zero: put the count 05 in C, the block address in HL, B = 00 as the zero counter; loop: MOV A, M, ORA A, JNZ skip, INR B, skip: INX H, DCR C, JNZ loop, then store B and HLT."}};
